<p>Those of you who read my blog regularly will know that some of my posts are rather niche. I have to warn you now that this one attains a whole new level of nichey-ness. However, in my usual spirit of ‘this is interesting to me’ and ‘writing it down helps me to remember it later’, here it is. This article might also be interesting to you if you:</p><ul><li>Use an external keyboard both with and without a built-in laptop keyboard;</li><li>Write code <em>and</em> need accented characters occasionally or constantly;</li><li>Use multiple operating systems (particularly macOS and Linux, but I suspect this stuff is relevant to Windows too);</li><li>Have a ZSA keyboard;</li><li>Use a Wayland window manager on NixOS.</li></ul><p>Still with me? Allons-y!</p><h3>The problem</h3><p>Keyboard layouts are baffling. Of course, lots of things in computing (and life) are baffling, but keyboard layouts seem to confound me in unique and brain-straining ways. I need to be clear about what I mean by ‘keyboard layouts’ here, as I have used (and will continue to use) that term in other ways in this blog. Today’s subject concerns the layout that your computer uses to determine which symbols are associated with particular single keypresses, with or without modifiers. So, for example, what character should be output if the <code>2</code> key is pressed with <code>Shift</code>? This is distinct from the other kind of keyboard layout that I have messed about with and written about here where we are deciding how the physical keys on any given keyboard are laid out. This determines which physical key outputs <code>2</code>. That’s about as far as I understand it even now, so experts among you may be tutting and rolling your eyes as you read this. Sorry.</p><p>Until recently, I didn’t have to think about these things often. I had some small niggles, but they weren’t bad enough or experienced often enough to force me to dig into it. However, I now have three computers in daily use: a MacBook Pro at work, and a desktop machine and Lenovo ThinkPad<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="https://www.rousette.org.uk/archives/the-one-true-keyboard-layout/#fn1" id="fnref1">[1]</a></sup> at home, both running Linux. I use all of the computers with my <a href="https://www.rousette.org.uk/archives/zsa-navigator/">ZSA Voyager keyboard</a>, and also use the built-in keyboards on the laptops. Oh, and I <em>also</em> use the ColemakDHm layout on my Voyager, but the standard Qwerty on the laptop keyboards.</p><p>The problem with all of these combinations was subtle but annoying inconsistency between the machines. One particularly aggravating thing was that macOS machines give you the <code>@</code> symbol when you press <code>Shift+2</code>, while PC machines give you <code>&quot;</code>. Conversely, PC machines have the <code>@</code> symbol as the shifted version of the <code>'</code> key instead, whereas macOS machines put the <code>&quot;</code> there. I have to say this little switcheroo has irritated me for years the moment I have to use a PC.</p><p>You might be thinking, well, don’t you use a programmable keyboard? Can’t you map those symbols where you expect them to be and at least have a consistent experience between platforms when you are using an external keyboard? You would think so, wouldn’t you, especially as I have a whole symbol layer on my keyboard with the symbols directly mapped (i.e. as <code>@</code>, not <code>Shift+2</code>). The Voyager can only change <em>where</em> a physical key is placed on the keyboard. This means that when I had my external keyboard attached to my Mac, that <code>@</code> symbol (which is apparently secretly <code>Shift+2</code>, no matter how I mapped it) would be output when I hit they key on my symbols layer, but would give me a <code>&quot;</code> with the keyboard attached to my Linux machines.</p><p>It’s particularly a problem for symbols and accented characters. As someone who does a fair bit of coding, I need easy access to symbols like <code>#</code>, <code>|</code>, <code>~</code> and so on. On a UK Mac keyboard, <code>Shift+3</code> gives you <code>£</code>, while <code>Alt+3</code> gives you <code>#</code>. I would much prefer these to be the other way around as I use the hash symbol much more than the £ symbol, amazingly enough. I found out that the Australian keyboad mapping is exactly the same as the UK one <em>except</em> that <code>#/£</code> mappings are switched the way I want them. So for years, I used the Australian keymap.</p><p>However, when I started connecting my Lenovo ThinkPad to my external keyboard, I couldn’t access either the <code>#</code> or <code>£</code> in the same places as with the Australian layout. The symbols I needed might be in any one of about three different places depending on the combination of machine and keyboard I happened to be using. It was chaos.</p><h3>Enter EurKEY 🇪🇺</h3><p>While trying to solve this riddle recently, I came across <a href="https://eurkey.steffen.bruentjen.eu/layout.html">EurKEY</a>. This fantastic bit of work enables easy access to programming symbols, accented characters and mathematical symbols, all from the same layout, by using layers created by pressing the <code>Shift</code>, <code>AltGr</code> or <code>Shift+AltGr</code> keys. There are versions for all platforms (including <a href="https://eurkey-macos.eu">macOS</a>, so the promise was that I would be able to press a combination of keys on any keyboard attached to any machine and get the same symbol.</p><p>If you go to either of the EurKEY pages linked in the previous paragraph, you will see a representation of a keyboard and you can press virtual keys to see how it works. Basically, each key encodes at least four different things. In the bottom left, you see the character that a plain keypress will give you, and above that (top left), the output by adding <code>Shift</code>. The output at bottom right is the result of pressing <code>Right Alt</code> (aka <code>AltGr</code>), then top right is <code>Shift+Right Alt</code>. In addition, there are ‘dead keys’ (shaded yellow on the EurKEY Next page). Activating these waits for you to release that key and hit another to ‘compose’ an accented character or mathematical symbol. It looks fiendishly complicated, but actually it is fairly simple once you get used to it, and most people are going to rarely need the stuff buried in the deeper layers of this mapping. The main thing is that once you’ve got used to it, it will work the same on any machine you connect it to.</p><p>It also has the <code>&quot;</code> symbol in the right place (don’t @ me) on the <code>2</code> key.</p><h3>Installing or switching to EurKEY</h3><p>On my Mac, this was a fairly easy operation: the webpage explains how to download and install the mapping, or you can install it with homebrew if that’s something you use anyway. You then go to the settings app, find the Input Sources part and add the new keymap. After rebooting the machine, you are ready to go.</p><p>I struggled a bit on Linux but only because EurKEY is apparently already installed on most modern systems, but it isn’t obvious that this is the case. As I mentioned, I use a Wayland Window Manager (Niri) rather than an X11-based system, so the way I set this up in Niri (using its native <code>*.kdl</code> format config file) is as follows:</p><div class="highlight"><pre class="language-conf" tabindex="0" data-language="conf"><code class="language-conf">input {
    keyboard {
        xkb {
            layout "eu,gb"
            rules ""
            variant ""
            options "lv3:ralt_switch"
        } 
        track-layout "global"
    }
}</code></pre></div><p>Note the <code>options</code> setting. I really wish that these codes were not so cryptic. The first time I set this up, the relevant changes to key positions (such as the <code>&quot;/@</code> switcheroo) happened, so I knew that it had been activated, but I couldn’t get to any of the <code>AltGr/Shift+AltGr</code> characters. After a lot of messing about with the utility <code>wev</code> to see what symbol my right alt key was sending, I realised that it was just sending a right alt, <em>not</em> <code>ISO_Level3_Shift</code> which is <code>AltGr</code>. More digging around in the xkb options (there are many) suggested that what I wanted was <code>lv3:ralt_switch</code> which means make the right alt key <code>ISO_Level3_Shift</code>. Once set, everything worked as expected.</p><p>I also initially set this at the host level instead, as a NixOS service. However, that did not seem to set it correctly in Niri, but perhaps I was doing something wrong.</p><p>I’ve left myself the safety net of having the GB layout set up as well so I can switch to it if needed, but so far I have been full-time EurKEY for a few days now, and I’m loving it.</p><h3>Configuring the Voyager</h3><p>The only piece remaining was to adjust my physical layout on the Voyager. ZSA has a template already for the EurKEY layout which sets up the labels for the keys for you to save a bit of time. The template is Qwerty layout so I had to shift them around into ColemakDHm layout. They also have a whole <a href="https://blog.zsa.io/eurkey-layout/">blog post about EurKEY</a>. I don’t know how I missed that, as I get and read their interesting newsletter every week.</p><div style="padding-top: 60%; position: relative;"><iframe src="https://configure.zsa.io/embed/voyager/layouts/9jlqK/latest/0" style="border: 0; height: 100%; left: 0; position: absolute; top: 0; width: 100%"></iframe></div><p>Initially, I changed the <code>Alt</code> key on my right half to be a right alt instead of a left alt. I quickly remembered that this is a terrible idea if you use Emacs, as many of the <code>M-</code> mappings stop working. Instead, I used outer keys on each half to output a one-shot right alt (left half) and a <code>Shift+Right Alt</code> (right half). This actually works much better, because I can tap the one-shot key then the other character whatever side it is on.</p><h3>Remaining tweaks: modifier order</h3><p>The other keyboard-based thing that can vary between platforms is the physical order of modifiers on the board, and their role in the OS and its software. For example, on a Mac keyboard, the modifiers are (from left to right) Shift, Control, Option (aka Alt), Command, whereas on my Lenovo keyboard they are Shift, Control, Win (aka Super or Meta or Command), Alt. On macOS, the Command key is most heavily used, sometimes in combination with Shift and/or Option, but Control isn’t used very much. On Linux, Control and Alt are used more and Super/Meta is not, so most Window Managers co-opt it as the main hotkey for controlling windows, launching apps and other window manager business. On my Mac, I use <a href="https://github.com/nikitabobko/AeroSpace">AeroSpace</a> as a tiling window manager, and map the Control key for these purposes. These differences were also driving me crazy, but as I can move keys around on my Voyager, I mapped the modifiers (from left to right) as Shift, Meta, Alt, Control, then mirrored that on the right half. I then swapped the Control and Command (Meta) keys in System Settings on my Mac, but only for the Voyager keyboard, not the built in one.</p><p>This means that my pinky finger always deals with Shift, my ring finger presses ‘the window manager hotkey’, middle finger is always Alt/Option, and my index finger handles the ‘main modifier key’. This works surprisingly well. Everything is very consistent when using my Voyager on either platform, and on my MacBook, the physical order of the keys is the same, so again it feels consistent.</p><p>My Lenovo ThinkPad has the keys in a completely different order, so that trips me up a bit when I use it. One of those runic xkb options (<code>ctrl:swap_lctrl_lalt_lwin</code> I think?) puts them in the same order as my Voyager, but when I tried that, it then applies the same translation to the Voyager, so you are back at square one! I don’t know how to restrict those xkb options to a specified device, but that’s the last remaining part. <a href="https://github.com/xremap/xremap">xremap</a> looks like it might be able to do both the modifier swapping and restrict it to a named device, but so far I have not been able to find the right magical incantation to get it to work. If anyone knows how, please let me know.</p><p>That’s about it. If you’ve got here, you deserve some kind of medal. Thank you for reading.</p><hr class="footnotes-sep"><section class="footnotes"><ol class="footnotes-list"><li id="fn1" class="footnote-item"><p>More to come later about this lovely little machine that I bought second-hand. <a href="https://www.rousette.org.uk/archives/the-one-true-keyboard-layout/#fnref1" class="footnote-backref">↩︎</a></p></li></ol></section>